+# Editorial review — Movement 005, Chapters 30–36
+
+## Review status and coverage
+
+**Disposition: REPAIR, then targeted recheck.** This is a strong, coherent movement with three localized continuity/logic defects. None requires restructuring a fight, cutaway, or chapter.
+
+This is an informed editorial read by a model family separate from the manuscript author. It is not a blind cold read, a human audience test, or author self-review. I read all seven supplied chapters in declared order, then checked them against the supplied edition brief, book map, state ledger, canon rules, universe bible, movement packet, numerical results, and source-overlap summary.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest catches decisively at Chapter 30's *“L., through the wall. Didn't.”* The line makes the new ability an ethical problem before it becomes a tactical tool. The Shield bout then pays off Cael's established method cleanly: two dated observations become a win, the opponent adapts, and the adaptation costs him his best attack. The movement's best emotional run is the supper in Chapter 32. Brom's jar, Lira's onions, the borrowed table, and the offer of footwork make the new three-person home feel chosen rather than declared.
+
+Interest slips mildly during the front-loaded schedule and purse explanation in Chapter 30, and again when Chapter 35 repeats Havel's full query and Coss's reply soon after Chapter 33 has presented both. The repetition is motivated by the viewpoint change, but a younger reader may feel the plot pause before Coss's decision becomes new information.
+
+Cael matters most because his central problem is now moral as well as technical: what may he learn about another person, who owns that knowledge, and what must he disclose? Vell is the movement's strongest supporting presence. Her public correction gives her record-keeping principles a human cost, while her guild history explains why the archive is not merely a hobby.
+
+I expect Cael to test the seam between Keth's second and third cuts, and I expect the watcher to remain ahead of Cael's range long enough to force a new use of the pulse. I would continue. The closing Keth realization is a precise forward hook, and the watcher, the impossible file, and *UNBOUND* remain distinct mysteries rather than collapsing into one answer.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
+The fluent thirteen-year-old lens is likely to respond strongly to the fights, the dinner, Lira and Brom teaching each other, and the clear costs of the new sense. The administrative cutaways are understandable, but the hierarchy of files, markers, sections, contacts, and supplementary notes asks for more sustained attention than the surrounding scenes. The movement meets the Reader Standard in content and moral framing, subject to the consent-continuity repair below.
+
+The adult genre-reader lens gets an additional pleasure from the movement's shared language of records: Cael's notebook, Vell's ledger, Havel's file, Coss's private log, and the old circuit book all ask who controls a fact after it is written. That motif is unusually well integrated with progression mechanics and institutional suspense.
+
+| Dimension | 13-year-old lens | Adult genre lens | Exact passage location and reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---:|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 7 | 8 | Ch. 30, opening, *“Dace put the main season up on the Monday”*: the board immediately changes the district, but the schedule and purse explanation delays the first intimate pressure. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9 | 9 | Ch. 35, read-back ending, *“Not as long as they can. Yet.”*; Ch. 36, closing, *“Between the second and the third”*: the movement gives local payoffs while preserving two clean next-movement questions. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | 9 | Ch. 31, Vell's chain correction; Ch. 35, the record window; Ch. 36, the witness-filled old ledger: record-keeping becomes action with social and political consequences. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7 | 8 | Ch. 33, Havel's Section Twelve sequence, and Ch. 35, Coss's receipt of the same query: each scene is locally clear, but repeated office language slows the through-line. The Ch. 32–34 consent contradiction also muddies the rules of Cael's conduct. | High |
+| Character attachment | 8 | 9 | Ch. 32, *“Being noticed. It's company, a bit.”* and Lira's footwork invitation; Ch. 31, Vell facing every person harmed by her correction. Agency and care cost each person something. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 9 | 9 | Ch. 32, the smoke, unknown pickles, three bowls of onions, and *“Are the two of you doing something invisible at my dinner table?”*: distinct comic habits deepen the relationships. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 8 | 8 | Ch. 30, the Shield's dropping shoulder and failed correction; Ch. 34, Lira refusing to catch and moving the Stone off his heels; Ch. 35, the long read-back contest. Position, adaptation, cost, and outcome remain trackable. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | 9 | Chs. 30, 33, 34, 35, and 36: six-pace stillness, crowd flood, walking yes/no, kindled/active distinction, false reading, watcher endurance, and the pulse form an earned learning chain. | High |
+| Connection | 8 | 9 | Ch. 31 turns the Shield observations into public evidence; Ch. 34 pays off Brom's diagnosis of Lira's hands; Ch. 36 pays off the oldest-books promise and seeds Keth. The consent rule is the one material dropped connection. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8 | 8 | Ch. 32's three-speaker supper and Ch. 34's corner coaching are easy to attribute by ear. Some long institutional paragraphs in Chs. 33 and 35 carry several abstract nouns before a concrete image returns. | Medium-high |
+
+The scores are editorial judgments, not audience measurements, and are not averaged into a release gate.
+
+## Continuity, story, and canon
+
+### Findings requiring repair
+
+1. **Cael's consent rule is contradicted by the next stages of training.** At the end of Chapter 32 he writes, *“A thing I can do to people without their knowing is a thing I have to ask before I do.”* In Chapter 33 he deliberately attends to the old man, the girl, and the smith one at a time to determine whether they are kindled, without asking. In Chapter 34 he establishes a nightly room sweep and reads late fighters without permission. The manuscript may intend a boundary between passive presence-sense and probing an individual, but it never states that distinction; the existing sentence is absolute. Because the supper explicitly names consent as the moral lesson, the immediate breach weakens Cael's integrity and the Reader Standard's requirement that wrong conduct be recognized as wrong.
+
+2. **Cael's response to Dace's warning protects only some of the people Dace has warned him about.** In Chapter 31 Dace explains that a dated book of names and habits can become a coercive list, including for fighters already known to the circuit. Cael then converts full names to private marks only where the fighter is *not* in Vell's ledger. That leaves ledgered fighters—the Shield and Keth among them—linked to detailed weaknesses in a portable private book. Either every fighter needs a private mark, or the narration needs to explain why ledger membership changes the threat model. As written, Cael's solution does not fully answer Dace's story, though the scene presents it as his considered correction.
+
+3. **Vell's guild chronology contains a small arithmetic contradiction.** In Chapter 36 she says she was Iron at nineteen and Bronze at twenty-one, that younger practitioners were brought to watch her, and *“I had a few years of that.”* She then says the disqualifying bout occurred when she was twenty-two. The Bronze/watchers interval is about one year, not a few. This conflicts internally and with the book map's intended early-twenties break, but it can be fixed in one phrase without moving any event.
+
+### Canon and state checks that pass
+
+- **Calendar and entry state:** Chapter 30 begins with the Monday main-season posting promised at the end of Movement 4. Cael's knees are improving but still complain on stairs; Brom is established in the alcove; Lira remains provisional Iron-equivalent.
+- **Bodies and consequences:** Cael's knee limits affect footwork choices, the Shield's rim leaves a short-lived forearm injury, Iron-adjacent use consistently produces the delayed headache band, and Lira carries the Stone's shoulder bruise through the bout. No injury disappears in a way that changes a later scene.
+- **Power limits:** The sense expands by specific conditions rather than convenience: stillness buys range; crowds collapse into one undifferentiated weight; walking permits only presence; finer distinctions require stillness and proximity; intent is not readable; pulses work briefly at close range and mostly return noise. Costs scale with duration and crowding. The watcher remains outside what Cael and Brom can penetrate.
+- **Ratings:** Lira's win is recorded as *“Provisional Iron-equivalent. One of two.”* Neither her rating nor Cael's is altered by Vell's correction chain. Cael remains assessed-Copper.
+- **Knowledge boundaries:** Lira and Brom receive information they are allowed to know. Cael tells Lira about the watcher on the same day. He tells nobody about the copied *UNBOUND* line. Havel and Coss never learn fragment facts, and Cael never learns the file facts.
+- **Reserved truths:** The movement does not state that Cael can deliberately integrate witnessed abilities; does not turn Vell's suspicion into evidence of classification falsification; does not conclude that *UNBOUND* names Cael; does not identify the registry sub-layer as the Architect's infrastructure; and gives neither the file nor the watcher will, intent, or a shared identity. The record window stays within the allowed facts.
+- **Movement promises:** The season economy, clean Shield win and dispute, correction chain, supper, Lira–Brom friendship, confirming bout, Iron-adjacent curriculum, three watcher sightings, Havel and Coss cutaways, record window, handling tour, and Keth seed all appear with the required outcomes.
+
+## Reader Standard
+
+The movement is suitable for the specified thirteen-year-old reader. It contains no profanity, obscenity, crude slang, blasphemous oath, sexual content, or innuendo. Violence is non-gory, spatially legible, and followed by pain, recovery, or social consequence. Betting is present as part of the circuit economy, but the dispute is framed around responsibility and honest records rather than glamorized easy gain.
+
+The moral center is especially strong in Vell accepting blame publicly, Cael declining to exploit the Shield's question, Lira telling Cael what disclosure she wants, Brom accepting instruction without pride, and Cael separating a sensed fact from an inferred intention. The consent inconsistency is therefore conspicuous: the book has taught the reader to expect Cael's stated ethical rules to govern his next action.
+
+## Protected wording and source reuse
+
+All protected wording assigned to this movement is present exactly where required:
+
+- Ch. 31: *“Good thing we don't share.”*
+- Ch. 32: *“You should be competing at Iron-equivalent.”* / *“I like him.”* / *“I thought you might.”*
+- Chs. 33 and 35: *“Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4”* and *“Follow standard procedure. Do not attempt to reclassify the monitoring level. File regular reports.”*
+- Ch. 35 record window: *“systemic protocol, origin: registry sub-layer.”*
+- Ch. 36: *“The oldest ones use different words for things.”*; the full marginal note, including brackets; Cael's bracketless copied first sentence; and *“The Compact does falsify things.” / “I know.”*
+
+The supplied overlap result reports **0 unprotected shared runs of eight or more words** and **6 protected runs**, so the source-reuse gate passes. The referenced `source-overlap.tsv` is currently zero bytes in the workspace; therefore the six allowed rows cannot be independently inspected from that artifact, and this conclusion relies on the supplied run summary.
+
+## Numerical formula alignment
+
+Coverage is Chapters 30–36: 7 chapters, 36,054 prose words, 2,525 measured sentences, 850 written paragraphs, and no fenced system-notice words. I used the supplied measurements and did not re-estimate them.
+
+Method: `editions/monroe-1.3/tools/formula_metrics.py`. It strips Markdown H1 lines and marked scene breaks, removes emphasis markers, excludes fenced notice text from sentence/readability statistics, treats each non-empty manuscript line as a written paragraph, and splits sentences with the documented punctuation/capitalization regex while protecting common abbreviations and ellipses. Syllables use a vowel-group heuristic with silent-*e* and *-le* adjustments; proper nouns and system terms are estimated like other words and can bias readability slightly. Scene counts are marked breaks plus chapter units. Paragraph and scene comparisons remain directional because their targets came from ASR pauses, not print typography.
+
+| Measure | Formula target | Accepted movement range, where supplied | Observed | Assessment |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| Sentence mean | 14.6 | 13–15.5 | 14.28 | Pass; close to target. |
+| Sentence median | 11 | — | 11 | Exact. |
+| Sentence population SD | ~26 | — | 11.47 | Material drift: the overall distribution is much less wide than the requested target, despite good tail shares. |
+| Sentences ≤5 words | 27.7% | up to ~34% | 28.5% | Pass. Short beats are not overused. |
+| Sentences ≥40 words | 3.3% | 2.5–4.5% | 3.6% | Pass. |
+| Written paragraph mean | 26.8 words, directional ASR proxy | — | 42.42 | High against the directional target, driven in part by sustained institutional narration. |
+| Written paragraph median | ~18 words, directional ASR proxy | up to ~30 | 28 | Within the accepted ceiling, but at its dense edge. |
+| Marked scene breaks / 10k | ~8.7 | — | 8.32 | Close. |
+| Words per scene | ~950 | 850–1,050 | 974.4 | Pass. |
+| Flesch Reading Ease | 72.3 | — | 89.5 | Much easier than the exact formula target. |
+| Flesch–Kincaid grade | 6.8 | 3.5–6 | 4.32 | Below the exact target but inside the edition's accepted working range. |
+
+The three primary working-range measures—sentence mean, forty-plus-word share, and words per scene—all pass. Median sentence length and short-sentence share also align. The large SD miss means the prose reaches the requested short and long thresholds without the extreme breadth implied by the source target. Readability is substantially easier than the exact target, but FK 4.32 remains inside the coordinator's accepted range and supports one-pass comprehension for the Reader Standard. I would not make vocabulary artificially harder merely to lower Flesch ease.
+
+The paragraph mean is the most visible remaining texture drift. Chapters 33 and 35 contain necessarily procedural material, and Chapter 36 contains archive explanation; their longer blocks make the movement feel denser than its grade score suggests. This is worth monitoring at cumulative-book scale, but it does not justify splitting coherent thoughts solely to hit an ASR-derived number.
+
+### Formula items not numerically measured
+
+- **Progression vocabulary density and third placement: UNMEASURED.** No project-specific lexicon count was supplied. By manual inspection, this middle-third movement follows the intended shape: progression terminology surfaces at actual turns—the flood, range, active/passive distinction, Lira's confirmation, false reading, watcher, and pulse—rather than becoming continuous exposition. That is qualitative plan adherence, not a rate.
+- **Development cadence: UNMEASURED as a rate.** Manual beats are well distributed: Cael revises his ethics of observation; Lira asks for an honest reading and converts the result into a bout decision; Brom admits that being noticed feels like company and accepts Lira's instruction; Vell corrects her own chain and discloses her guild break; Dace defines record stewardship; Havel chooses a careful query; Coss places his name on an upward warning. The distribution reaches six supporting characters rather than concentrating everything in one deuteragonist.
+- **POV word share: UNMEASURED.** Cael anchors the movement except for the Havel cutaway in Chapter 33 and the Coss cutaway plus impersonal record window in Chapter 35. Those are purposeful, bounded departures across two named secondary viewpoints. The book-level 87/13 allocation and spread across four to five characters cannot be confirmed from this movement alone.
+- **Secondary signals (-ly suffixes, reporting verbs, combat lexicon, fillers, repeated phrases): UNMEASURED.** No reproducible counts or lexicons were supplied, so no precise compliance claim is made.
+
+## Strengths to carry forward
+
+- The progression curriculum is exemplary: every refinement answers a concrete failure, and the pulse emerges from trying the wrong method rather than from arbitrary escalation.
+- The movement's record motif unifies domestic trust, combat study, circuit justice, bureaucracy, and ancient history without making them the same mystery.
+- Lira and Brom develop a relationship independent of Cael. Their exchange of honest measurement for footwork instruction makes the found family reciprocal.
+- Both fights deserve their space. The Shield adapts after one exchange; Lira wins by refusing the position in which her weakness matters. Neither contest should be shortened for metric reasons.
+- The reserved reveals remain genuinely reserved. *UNBOUND*, the Level 4 marker, and the watcher create three kinds of pressure without the narration prematurely explaining any of them.
+
+## Repair brief
+
+1. **Clarify the consent boundary of Iron-adjacent sensing.**
+   - **Location:** Ch. 32, final Log rule; Ch. 33, market tests on the old man, girl, and smith; Ch. 34, nightly sweeps.
+   - **Observed issue:** Cael writes an absolute ask-first rule, then reads strangers without asking and without recognizing a breach.
+   - **Effect on the reader:** It makes a newly earned moral insight look forgotten and weakens trust in Cael's honesty, especially under the thirteen-year-old Reader Standard.
+   - **Proposed scope:** A localized same-author repair defining what requires consent—most plausibly, targeted reading beyond public presence—and revising the Log line and first public test so that every later sweep follows the stated rule. Do not remove the training sequence or watcher detection.
+   - **Strength to preserve:** The supper's warmth, Brom's *“Being noticed”* admission, and Cael's willingness to name his own wrong.
+
+2. **Make the notebook-security response answer Dace's actual warning.**
+   - **Location:** Ch. 31, from Dace's *“Good thing we don't share”* conversation through Cael's evening recoding of names.
+   - **Observed issue:** Cael anonymizes only fighters absent from Vell's ledger, leaving ledgered fighters attached to detailed exploitable weaknesses without explaining why that is safe.
+   - **Effect on the reader:** The solution feels incomplete and undercuts both Dace's example and Cael's reputation for following evidence to its practical consequence.
+   - **Proposed scope:** One small logic repair: anonymize all fighter identities in the private book, or explicitly establish a different protection for ledgered names that prevents the notebook becoming the list Dace fears.
+   - **Strength to preserve:** Dace's scouted-fighter story, the principle that records belong partly to the people in them, and Cael's choice not to destroy factual observations.
+
+3. **Correct Vell's one-year/few-years chronology.**
+   - **Location:** Ch. 36, Vell's guild history: *“Bronze at twenty-one”*, *“I had a few years of that”*, and *“I was twenty-two.”*
+   - **Observed issue:** The stated ages allow roughly one year of Bronze standing before the disqualifying bout, not a few years.
+   - **Effect on the reader:** A simple arithmetic snag interrupts an otherwise persuasive disclosure and creates avoidable doubt about the mapped timeline.
+   - **Proposed scope:** One phrase only—change the duration wording, without moving her ages, the bout, or her early-twenties break from the guild.
+   - **Strength to preserve:** Vell's restrained delivery and the revelation that she keeps the archive because official recognition once erased something she truly did.

